\title{Sound Small-Scale Automation for Min-Plus Algebra,\\Primality, and Spectral Bounds}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 25, 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop three self-contained, theorem-driven procedures for recurring mathematical tasks. The first normalizes a useful class of min-plus expressions using distributivity, idempotence, associativity, and neutral-element rules. The second decides concrete primality statements by exhaustive trial division and proves that the Boolean result is equivalent to the standard definition of a prime natural number. The third bounds real eigenvalues of finite real matrices using absolute row sums and a maximal coordinate of a nonzero eigenvector. The results include two-sided tropical distributivity, three-way distribution and absorption; a correct primality decision method certifying that $97$ is prime and $91$ is composite; and the estimate $-B\leq\lambda\leq B$ whenever every absolute row sum is at most $B$. Each procedure has an explicit specification, soundness argument, algorithm, complexity analysis, and applications.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Routine mathematical subproblems often invite specialized procedures: nested minima and sums are simplified, concrete integers are classified as prime or composite, and eigenvalues are bounded without being computed. A useful procedure should have an explicit mathematical contract. Its input domain must be defined, its output must have a precise interpretation, and a theorem must connect the operational test to that interpretation.

The three procedures studied here are deliberately small. Min-plus simplification uses a finite rewrite basis. Primality is decided by searching every potential proper divisor below the input. A spectral estimate reduces an eigenvalue bound to uniform absolute row sums. Their scope is modest enough that the soundness arguments can be understood from first principles, yet they model a general methodology: computational work should be organized around transparent mathematical invariants.

Throughout, $\N$ and $\R$ denote the natural and real numbers. For $x\in\R$, $|x|$ is its absolute value. For $d,n\in\N$, $d\mid n$ means that $d$ divides $n$. For an $n\times n$ real matrix $A=(A_{ij})$ and $v\in\R^n$, $(Av)_i=\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}v_j$.

\section{Min-plus simplification}

\begin{definition}[Min-plus operations]
For $a,b\in\R$, define tropical addition and multiplication by
\[
a\tadd b=\min(a,b),\qquad a\tmul b=a+b.
\]
\end{definition}
These operations encode choice by minimum and sequential composition by ordinary addition, as in shortest-path and scheduling problems.

\begin{theorem}[Left tropical distributivity]
For all $a,b,c\in\R$,
\[
a\tmul(b\tadd c)=(a\tmul b)\tadd(a\tmul c).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expanding the definitions gives $a+\min(b,c)=\min(a+b,a+c)$. Translation by $a$ preserves order, so both sides select corresponding terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Right tropical distributivity]
For all $a,b,c\in\R$,
\[
(a\tadd b)\tmul c=(a\tmul c)\tadd(b\tmul c).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Commute ordinary addition, apply left distributivity, and commute the two resulting sums.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Tropical idempotence]
For every $a\in\R$, $a\tadd a=a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is $\min(a,a)=a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sound rewrite basis]
Replacing a subexpression by the opposite side of any of
\[
\begin{aligned}
a\tmul(b\tadd c)&=(a\tmul b)\tadd(a\tmul c),\\
(a\tadd b)\tmul c&=(a\tmul c)\tadd(b\tmul c),\\
a\tadd a&=a,\qquad a\tmul0=0\tmul a=a
\end{aligned}
\]
preserves the value of the whole expression.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The first three identities are the preceding results; the last two are ordinary additive neutral-element laws. Equality is preserved in every expression context.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three-way tropical distribution]
For all $a,b,c,d\in\R$,
\[
a\tmul((b\tadd c)\tadd d)
=(a\tmul b)\tadd((a\tmul c)\tadd(a\tmul d)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply left distributivity to the outer choice and again to $a\tmul(b\tadd c)$, then use associativity of minimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical absorption]
For all $a,b\in\R$,
\[
(a\tmul b)\tadd\bigl(a\tmul(b\tadd b)\bigr)=a\tmul b.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Idempotence gives $b\tadd b=b$; idempotence again removes the two identical outer alternatives.
\end{proof}

These rules soundly expand products over finite minima, remove immediate duplication, and simplify neutral additions. They do not yet provide a unique canonical form for every min-plus polynomial. Aggressive distribution may also cause exponential expression growth, just as ordinary expansion of products of sums can.

\section{Exhaustive trial division}

\begin{definition}[Proper trial divisor]
For $n\in\N$, a number $d$ is a proper trial divisor when $2\leq d<n$ and $d\mid n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Proper-divisor search]
The proper-divisor search for $n$ inspects $d\in\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$ and returns true exactly when an inspected $d$ satisfies $2\leq d$ and $d\mid n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Trial-primality test]
The test returns true exactly when $2\leq n$ and the proper-divisor search returns false.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Prime]
A natural number $n$ is prime when $2\leq n$ and no $m$ satisfying $2\leq m<n$ divides $n$.
\end{definition}
This bounded-divisor definition is equivalent to saying that the positive divisors are exactly $1$ and $n$.

\begin{lemma}[Search characterization]
The proper-divisor search for $n$ returns true if and only if there exists $d\in\N$ such that
\[
2\leq d<n\quad\text{and}\quad d\mid n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A finite existential search succeeds precisely when one enumerated member satisfies its predicate. Membership in $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ is equivalent to $d<n$; combining this with the predicate gives the claim in both directions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness of exhaustive trial primality]
For every $n\in\N$, the trial-primality test returns true if and only if $n$ is prime.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If the test returns true, then $2\leq n$ and the search returns false. Any $m$ with $2\leq m<n$ and $m\mid n$ would make the search true by the preceding lemma, a contradiction. Hence $n$ is prime. Conversely, if $n$ is prime, then $2\leq n$. A successful search would supply a forbidden proper divisor, so the search is false and the test is true.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The number $97$ is prime.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
No integer $d$ with $2\leq d<97$ divides $97$. Thus the exhaustive test returns true, and correctness transfers this result to primality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The number $91$ is not prime.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The witness $7$ satisfies $2\leq7<91$ and $91=7\cdot13$; hence the search succeeds and the primality test returns false.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The natural numbers $97$ and $91$ are distinct.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Equality would transfer primality from $97$ to $91$, contradicting the previous corollary.
\end{proof}

A composite result carries a short witness, namely one proper divisor. A prime result under this method records the absence of witnesses throughout a finite range. The algorithm performs at most $n-2$ divisibility tests, so it uses $O(n)$ tests and $O(1)$ auxiliary storage. Testing only through $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor$ is faster but requires the additional theorem that every composite $n$ has a factor no greater than $\sqrt n$.

\section{Absolute row-sum spectral estimates}
For $A\in\R^{n\times n}$ define
\[
r_i(A)=\sum_{j=1}^n|A_{ij}|,
\qquad
\|A\|_\infty=\max_{1\leq i\leq n}r_i(A).
\]
A real number $\lambda$ is a real eigenvalue if $Av=\lambda v$ for some nonzero $v\in\R^n$.

\begin{lemma}[Positive maximal coordinate]
If $v\in\R^n$ is nonzero, then an index $i_0$ exists such that
\[
0<|v_{i_0}|\quad\text{and}\quad |v_i|\leq|v_{i_0}|\ \text{for every }i.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The finite set of coordinate magnitudes has a maximum. If that maximum were zero, every coordinate would be zero, contrary to $v\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A row bounds a real eigenvalue]
Let $A\in\R^{n\times n}$, $\lambda\in\R$, and $v\neq0$ satisfy $Av=\lambda v$. Then some row $i$ satisfies
\[
|\lambda|\leq r_i(A)=\sum_{j=1}^n|A_{ij}|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $i_0$ from the lemma. The $i_0$-th coordinate equation and the triangle inequality give
\[
|\lambda|\,|v_{i_0}|
\leq\sum_{j=1}^n|A_{i_0j}|\,|v_j|.
\]
Maximality of $|v_{i_0}|$ bounds the right side by
\[
\sum_{j=1}^n|A_{i_0j}|\,|v_{i_0}|
=r_{i_0}(A)|v_{i_0}|.
\]
Since $|v_{i_0}|>0$, cancellation yields $|\lambda|\leq r_{i_0}(A)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform row-sum bound]
Under the preceding hypotheses, if $r_i(A)\leq B$ for every row $i$, then $|\lambda|\leq B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A witnessing row satisfies $|\lambda|\leq r_i(A)\leq B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Symmetric interval estimate]
Under the same assumptions,
\[
-B\leq\lambda\leq B.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For real $\lambda$, the displayed conjunction is equivalent to $|\lambda|\leq B$.
\end{proof}

For a dense matrix, computing all row sums takes $O(n^2)$ arithmetic operations and can use $O(1)$ auxiliary memory when rows are streamed. For a sparse matrix with $m$ nonzero entries, it takes $O(m+n)$. The certificate consists only of the row sums and their maximum; no eigenvalue or eigenvector need be computed.

\section{Examples and applications}
For a common prefix cost $4$ and alternatives $7,2,5$, tropical distribution gives
\[
4\tmul((7\tadd2)\tadd5)
=11\tadd(6\tadd9)=6.
\]
This expresses the dynamic-programming principle that a common cost can move across a choice without changing the optimum. Duplicate alternatives disappear by tropical absorption.

For number classification, the search at $97$ exhausts $2$ through $96$ without finding a divisor. At $91$ it stops with the witness $7$. The former is a finite absence certificate; the latter is the explicit factorization $91=7\cdot13$.

Consider the symmetric tridiagonal matrix
\[
A=\begin{pmatrix}
2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&2
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Its absolute row sums are $3,4,3$. Every real eigenvalue therefore lies in $[-4,4]$. Symmetry ensures that the entire spectrum is real; its eigenvalues are approximately $0.586$, $2$, and $3.414$. The bound is inexpensive but may be conservative because absolute values discard cancellation.

The three procedures support route planning and scheduling, elementary number classification, and matrix stability screening. They also exhibit a shared architecture: define semantics, carry out finite computation, and connect the two by a soundness theorem.

\section{Limitations and future work}
Local tropical rewriting does not yet decide all identities or guarantee a unique normal form. A natural extension is a syntax for finite min-plus polynomials together with evaluation-preserving normalization and canonical antichain forms.

Exhaustive trial division scales poorly. Restricting the search to $d\leq\sqrt n$ requires a complementary-factor lemma and would reduce the number of tests. The same certificate approach can cover concrete divisibility, coprimality, congruences, and bounded Diophantine problems.

The row-sum bound is centered at zero and ignores diagonal location and cancellation. Extensions include complex matrices, arbitrary finite index sets, Gershgorin discs centered at $A_{ii}$, column sums, weighted norms, and strict diagonal-dominance tests for nonsingularity.

Finally, procedures can be composed by recognizing problem shape: min-plus expressions invite rewriting, concrete primality claims invite finite search, and eigenvalue goals with uniform row bounds invite the spectral theorem. A useful dispatcher should retain an explanatory trace identifying the theorem and evidence used. Performance should be evaluated on representative families by measuring success, expression growth, and execution time.

\section{Conclusion}
Min-plus rewriting rests on distributivity and idempotence. Exhaustive trial division rests on an exact equivalence between finite search and primality. Absolute row sums bound real eigenvalues through a maximal coordinate and the triangle inequality. Each procedure has a transparent input, output, proof of soundness, and complexity profile. The broader principle is simple: a rewrite should expose an equality, a decision should correspond to a logical characterization, and a bound should display the inequalities supporting it.

\end{document}

